WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.580
Fair warning.

2
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.471
This is an embellished
fictional dramatization

3
00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:07.301
that may or may not be
based on true stories.

4
00:00:07.321 --> 00:00:09.885
The narrator is a guy named Charlie Hack.

5
00:00:09.905 --> 00:00:12.715
Any resemblance to me
is mostly coincidental.

6
00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:17.452
Names, characters, companies and events
are fictitious, or used fictitiously.

7
00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:19.372
Silicon Valley C Players.

8
00:00:19.392 --> 00:00:22.300
CS150. One of the hardest
classes on campus.

9
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.700
Three of us were building a MIDI music
machine, taking turns in the lab, all night.

10
00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.881
And at night, I dreamed in green.

11
00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:34.131
Black screens, glowing
green lines, like Tron.

12
00:00:34.151 --> 00:00:38.764
AND gates, XOR gates, NAND gates,
waiting to be burned into a chip.

13
00:00:38.784 --> 00:00:41.279
All three of us had the same dream.

14
00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:43.444
Real life bled into our dreams.

15
00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:46.491
College was not all dreams. Math 1A.

16
00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:50.856
The professor said we were
all stupid American kids.

17
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:54.232
First midterm: Cs and Ds. For everybody.

18
00:00:54.252 --> 00:00:56.044
No curve. It hit hard.

19
00:00:56.064 --> 00:00:59.923
A music elective, where
everyone got an A+.

20
00:00:59.943 --> 00:01:02.744
CS61B: an A+, top of the class.

21
00:01:02.764 --> 00:01:04.684
Algorithms: a B-minus.

22
00:01:04.704 --> 00:01:07.097
The MIDI machine mostly
worked. I got an A.

23
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:09.893
My lab partner, a brilliant,
straight-A electrical

24
00:01:09.913 --> 00:01:11.922
engineering student, got an A-minus.

25
00:01:11.942 --> 00:01:15.705
I think it was probably the first
A-minus anyone had ever given him.

26
00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:16.473
He was livid.

27
00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:18.776
I'm not saying I was smarter than he was.

28
00:01:18.796 --> 00:01:19.708
He was brilliant.

29
00:01:19.728 --> 00:01:23.321
It's all relative, how smart you are.

30
00:01:23.341 --> 00:01:26.500
I just kept going. And
I kept doing my best.

31
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.168
I interned at a startup
while still in school,

32
00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:31.439
then took my first full-time job there.

33
00:01:31.459 --> 00:01:34.165
Seventy thousand dollars,
right out of college.

34
00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:36.322
Ninety thousand after the first year.

35
00:01:36.342 --> 00:01:39.388
Later, the company was
acquired by a firm from Berlin.

36
00:01:39.408 --> 00:01:41.015
This was the dot-com boom.

37
00:01:41.035 --> 00:01:43.185
Money was flowing in San Francisco.

38
00:01:43.205 --> 00:01:44.933
I was there at ground level.

39
00:01:44.953 --> 00:01:47.404
Another luck and experience I got to see.

40
00:01:47.424 --> 00:01:50.474
Also in the book: East
Berlin. Beer in canoes.

41
00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:52.433
A famous founder's apartment.

42
00:01:52.453 --> 00:01:55.372
Drunk nights in San
Francisco. All-nighters.

43
00:01:54.477 --> 00:01:58.180
A three-million-dollar
exit at twenty-five.

44
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:10.780
I.

45
00:02:01.642 --> 00:02:04.597
Which is funny, because
I should be the saddest.

46
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:08.505
I. came after the code writers first. I.

47
00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:10.780
now writes essentially all of the code.

48
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:22.943
So gear your software engineering
practices around that.

49
00:02:22.963 --> 00:02:23.068
I.

50
00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:28.037
The book begins with that
green-screen dream. I.

51
00:02:28.057 --> 00:02:29.266
It's free. It's open.

52
00:02:29.286 --> 00:02:32.057
And when version one is
ready, if you were around

53
00:02:32.077 --> 00:02:35.073
for any of it, you get to tell your side.

54
00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:37.027
The story is still being written.

55
00:02:37.047 --> 00:02:39.316
Silicon Valley C Players. Coming in 2027.
